<body class="my-content-page-1">
  <header><div><a href="@{siteAddress}/">Home</a></div></header>
  <main>
    <div class="left-column"></div>
    <div class="center-column">
      <h1>Arcadia Visuals Programming Language</h1>
      <h2>Specification (draft)</h2>
      <p>Arcadia VPL is a public, typed, three-address, virtual-register language for visual
      processing. Authors and tools describe typed operations and explicit control-flow graphs.
      The canonical in-memory representation is <code>Arcadia.VPL.IR.*</code>. A future higher-level
      language belongs to a separate module and produces canonical VPL.</p>
      <p>The words <em>shall</em> and <em>must</em> express requirements. This draft specifies the
      canonical object model, verifier contract, and implemented version-1 textual format. The
      grammar and public reading/writing/publication APIs are specified in sections 10 and 11.</p>
      <nav aria-label="Specification contents">
        <ol>
          <li><a href="#status">Scope and status</a></li>
          <li><a href="#lexical-translation">Lexical translation</a></li>
          <li><a href="#model">Modules, stages, functions, and blocks</a></li>
          <li><a href="#types">Types</a></li>
          <li><a href="#values">Values and mutable registers</a></li>
          <li><a href="#storage">Storage references</a></li>
          <li><a href="#instructions">Instructions</a></li>
          <li><a href="#control-flow">Control flow</a></li>
          <li><a href="#verification">Verification</a></li>
          <li><a href="#text-format">Canonical text format</a></li>
          <li><a href="#publication">Text APIs and verified publication</a></li>
          <li><a href="#glsl-backend">GLSL backend</a></li>
          <li><a href="#pending">Remaining specification work</a></li>
        </ol>
      </nav>

      <h2 id="status">1. Scope and status</h2>
      <p>VPL's canonical types, values, instructions, terminators, and module containers are
      implemented. The initial <code>IR.Verifier</code> checks directly constructed modules,
      including operand types, block ownership, root and derived storage bindings, register
      definite assignment, stage entries/linkage, output definedness, and acyclic helper effects.
      Text reading/writing, immutable publication, and a GLSL 420 core backend are implemented.
      The engine material/output path consumes published canonical modules and artifacts.</p>
      <p>Legacy AST/symbol implementation remains scheduled for retirement; it is not another
      public VPL language surface or part of the active shader emitter. This specification describes simple operations and
      branches, not nested source expressions or structured if/while/for syntax.</p>

      <h2 id="lexical-translation">2. Lexical translation</h2>
      <p>Canonical textual VPL shall select reusable productions and translation rules from
      <a href="@{siteAddress}/specifications/common-lexical-translations/index.html">Common Lexical
      Translations</a>. The lexical input alphabet is the set of Unicode code points. Shared rules
      are incorporated by reference; VPL-specific additions or refinements shall be stated explicitly.</p>
      <pre><code>name                : Lexical.Name
numberLiteral       : Lexical.Number
stringLiteral       : Lexical.String
booleanLiteral      : Lexical.Boolean
voidLiteral         : Lexical.Void
whitespace          : Lexical.Whitespace
endOfLine           : Lexical.EndOfLine
lineContinuation    : Lexical.LineContinuation
singleLineComment   : Lexical.SingleLineComment
multiLineComment    : Lexical.MultiLineComment
period              : Lexical.Period
leftParenthesis     : Lexical.LeftParenthesis
rightParenthesis    : Lexical.RightParenthesis
leftCurlyBracket    : Lexical.LeftCurlyBracket
rightCurlyBracket   : Lexical.RightCurlyBracket
leftSquareBracket   : Lexical.LeftSquareBracket
rightSquareBracket  : Lexical.RightSquareBracket
colon               : Lexical.Colon
comma               : Lexical.Comma
startOfInput        : Lexical.StartOfInput
endOfInput          : Lexical.EndOfInput</code></pre>
      <p>VPL shall define its own lexical start symbol and word alternatives from selected common
      and VPL-specific productions. The common specification supplies no universal word production.
      A production's availability does not imply that it is permitted in every operand position,
      or that VPL has a corresponding value type.</p>
      <p>The selected word alternatives, register/label spelling, boundaries, and disambiguation
      are defined in section 10. The aliases above name available common productions, not a promise
      that every punctuation category is a version-1 word. VPL and PDL are independent languages:
      PDL's syntax and lexical refinements are not
      inherited. Possible grammar convergence is deferred.</p>

      <h2 id="model">3. Modules, stages, functions, and blocks</h2>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Object</th><th>Contents</th><th>Requirements</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><code>Module</code></td><td>uniforms, vertex stage, fragment stage</td><td>Both stage objects shall have their designated stage kinds.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>Stage</code></td><td>kind, ordered interface list, functions, entryFunction</td><td>Names are unique within their collections. The selected entry is a same-stage parameterless void function.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>Function</code></td><td>name, return type, ordered parameters, ordered blocks</td><td>The function shall have at least one block. Its first block is the entry block.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>Block</code></td><td>ordered instructions, one terminator</td><td>A block belongs to exactly one function and appears once in that function's list.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>Branches target block objects in the same function. There is no implicit fall-through.
      Instructions execute in list order, followed by the block's terminator. NULL terminators may
      be used while building a graph, but are invalid in a verified function.</p>
      <p>Function parameters are reference descriptors in ordered parameter slots, supplied by
      value at invocation. Calls shall refer to functions in the calling stage. Each stage explicitly
      selects one function in its own list as entry; neither a main name nor list order selects it.
      Ordinary helpers may have parameters and non-void returns. Function/block objects shall not
      be shared between stages. Stage_create creates an unfinished stage with NULL entry, and
      Stage_createWithEntryFunction can supply it at construction; verification requires it.</p>
      <h3 id="interface-linkage">3.1 Interface linkage</h3>
      <p>Every ordinary fragment input, whether used or not, requires exactly one ordinary vertex
      output. An explicit input location matches the same explicit output location, regardless of
      names. If both locations are -1, match by exact case-sensitive name. There is no mixed-mode
      inference or name fallback from an explicit location. Types must be equal, including array
      lengths and nominal record identity. Non-None Color/TexCoord tags must agree when both sides
      specify one. None leaves that tag unspecified, not the type. Unconsumed vertex outputs are
      allowed; vertex inputs and fragment outputs are not linkage candidates.</p>
      <p>Position is a floating vec4 vertex output or fragment input with location -1. It is not
      a varying match: fragment Position is supplied by rasterization. FragmentColor is a floating
      vec4 fragment output. Required declarations and definedness are specified in section 6.3.</p>

      <h2 id="types">4. Types</h2>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Shape</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><code>VoidType</code></td><td>No value; permitted as a function return type.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>ScalarType</code></td><td>Boolean, Integer, or Floating element kind.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>VectorType</code></td><td>One scalar element kind and 2, 3, or 4 components.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>MatrixType</code></td><td>Floating elements, 2 to 4 rows, and 2 to 4 columns.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>SamplerType</code></td><td>The currently supported kind is Sampler2D.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>RecordType</code></td><td>A name and ordered, uniquely named fields, each with a value type.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>ArrayType</code></td><td>A value element type and a positive fixed element count.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>Void shall not be a register, parameter, uniform, interface, record-field, or array-element
      value type. Recursive value types are invalid. Empty records are representable. Unsized arrays
      are not supported by the initial verifier, including an <code>ArrayUniform</code> with count zero.</p>
      <p>Scalar kinds, vector element kinds and sizes, matrix dimensions, sampler kinds, and array
      element types and lengths participate in structural type equality. Records have nominal
      identity: distinct record type objects are not interchangeable merely because their names or
      fields match. Textual named-type binding must preserve this identity.</p>
      <p>The current scalar constant representation uses Boolean values, signed 32-bit integer
      values, and IEEE binary32 floating values. Constants must be finite; positive and negative
      zero are distinct stored bit patterns preserved by text round trips. Additional numeric kinds
      and target resource limits are extensions.</p>
      <h3 id="numeric-contract">4.1 Initial numeric contract</h3>
      <p>Integer arithmetic is signed 32-bit. Division truncates toward zero. Signed overflow,
      division by zero, and minimum-integer divided by -1 have undefined execution behavior;
      statically explicit constant zero divisors and constant minimum/-1 divisions are rejected.
      The verifier checks types and those explicit invalid divisions, not general range proofs.</p>
      <p>Floating storage is binary32. Literal conversion rounds nearest-even, preserving signed
      zero; finite underflow may yield subnormals or signed zero. Arithmetic may produce infinities
      and NaNs even though literals cannot encode them. A backend must declare its rounding,
      subnormal, and contraction behavior; version 1 does not promise bit-identical arithmetic across
      targets. Equal treats signed zeros as equal and NaNs as unequal; NotEqual is its complement,
      and ordered comparisons with NaN are false. No algebraic rewrite may silently change these
      control-flow comparison results. Section 12 defines the implemented GLSL target profile.</p>

      <h2 id="values">5. Values and mutable registers</h2>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Value form</th><th>Fields</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><code>RegisterValue</code></td><td>index, type</td><td>A function-local, statically typed, mutable virtual register.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>ConstantValue</code></td><td>type, boxed literal</td><td>A Boolean/Integer32/Real32 scalar literal matching its explicit type.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>ReferenceValue</code></td><td>storageKind, slot, type</td><td>A root storage descriptor, also the base class of derived reference descriptors.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>MemberReferenceValue</code></td><td>base reference, fieldName, inherited type</td><td>An access path to a record field, used by loads and stores.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>IndexReferenceValue</code></td><td>base reference, index value, inherited type</td><td>An access path to an array element, used by loads and stores.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>Computation operands shall be registers or scalar constants. A storage reference is not
      implicitly loaded when used in an arithmetic operation, constructor, call, or return; a
      <code>LoadInstruction</code> is required. Aggregate values are loaded, copied, or constructed
      rather than boxed as scalar constants.</p>
      <h3 id="register-declarations">5.1 Declarations and assignment</h3>
      <p>All registers shall be declared exactly once in a prefix of <code>DeclareLocalInstruction</code>
      objects in the entry block, before the first executable operation. These declarations are
      function-wide metadata and do not initialize values. Register indices need not be dense.</p>
      <p>A register keeps its declared type but may be assigned repeatedly, including within loops
      and on different branch paths. Every descriptor using the same index in a function shall
      agree with that declared type. Copy, load, arithmetic, construction, extraction, non-void
      calls, and whole-local stores can assign registers. An instruction reads its inputs before
      assigning its destination, so <code>r0 = add r0, r1</code> requires both old input values.</p>
      <h3 id="definite-assignment">5.2 Definite assignment</h3>
      <p>Every reachable register read shall be preceded by assignment on every incoming control-flow
      path. The invocation begins with no assigned local registers. At a join, the incoming assigned
      set is the intersection of predecessor outgoing sets; a write adds its destination to that set.
      Loops are analyzed to a fixed point. Assignment only on a back-edge cannot justify a read on
      the first iteration.</p>
      <p>The verifier considers both successors of a conditional branch, even for a constant
      condition. It validates types and graph structure in unreachable blocks too, while checking
      register availability only in executable prefixes reachable from entry. Non-returning calls
      cut continuation as specified in section 6.3. There are no phi operations or
      block parameters in this mutable-register model.</p>

      <h2 id="storage">6. Storage references</h2>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Namespace</th><th>Slot meaning</th><th>Permissions</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><code>StorageKind_Local</code></td><td>A declared register's numeric index.</td><td>Read/write; a whole-local store assigns that register, and a load requires definite assignment.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>StorageKind_Parameter</code></td><td>Position in the current function's parameter list.</td><td>Read/write invocation-local parameter storage; writes do not alter the caller's argument storage.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>StorageKind_Uniform</code></td><td>Position in the module's uniform list.</td><td>Read-only.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>StorageKind_StageIO</code></td><td>Position in the current stage's interface list, including inputs and outputs.</td><td>Inputs are read-only; outputs are writable.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>A reference's type shall equal the stored value type of its bound slot. Parameter descriptors
      shall use the Parameter namespace and their list positions. The legacy two-argument reference
      constructor defaults to the Local namespace; canonical producers can use
      <code>ReferenceValue_createWithStorage</code> to specify a namespace explicitly.</p>
      <p>Uniform names shall be unique. Scalar uniforms carry a value type, record uniforms carry a
      nominal record type, and array uniforms carry an element type and positive count. A whole
      array or record can be loaded into a register. <code>MemberInstruction</code> and
      <code>IndexInstruction</code> extract values; they do not create references to sub-storage.
      Loads and stores through sub-storage instead use the derived reference descriptors below.</p>
      <p>Interface directions, semantics, and locations are checked within each stage. Location -1
      denotes an unspecified location. Names shall be unique, and locations and non-None semantics
      shall not repeat within one direction. The semantic tags are None, Position, Color, TexCoord,
      and FragmentColor. Position is a floating vec4 vertex output or fragment
      input; FragmentColor is a floating vec4 fragment output. Output initialization and
      interprocedural storage effects use section 6.3.</p>

      <h3 id="derived-references">6.1 Derived storage references</h3>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Descriptor</th><th>Base and selector</th><th>Stored value type</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><code>MemberReferenceValue</code></td><td>A reference to record storage and an existing field name.</td><td>The selected field's declared type.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>IndexReferenceValue</code></td><td>A reference to array storage and an integer register or scalar constant.</td><td>The array element type.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>These descriptors derive from <code>ReferenceValue</code>, with kind Member or Index and
      inherited slot zero. Their inherited type shall match the selected stored value type. A base
      reference may itself be derived, so paths can select an array field, an element of that array,
      and a field of that element, or index multiple nested arrays. Paths shall be acyclic, and
      object class, kind, selector, and type shall agree. Parameter declarations still require root
      Parameter descriptors, not derived references with a forged Parameter kind.</p>
      <p>A path inherits read/write permissions from its root. A field or element of a uniform or
      stage input remains read-only; the derived descriptor cannot grant writable access. A member
      selector requires record storage, not a vector swizzle or a computed record value. An index
      selector requires array storage and an integer operand. Literal indices must be nonnegative
      and smaller than the containing array's count at each selection level.</p>
      <p>Reference descriptors describe access paths, not frozen pointer values. A mutable index
      register is read when a load or store uses the path, rather than when the descriptor is
      constructed. All index reads and storage-address selection occur before a load assigns its
      result or a store writes its value. Reusing a path after changing an index register can select
      a different element. Dynamic index registers must be definitely assigned at each use;
      out-of-bounds dynamic access has undefined execution behavior, with no implicit clamp or wrap.</p>
      <p>A load through a derived reference produces the selected stored value. A store through it
      requires an exactly matching value and updates that sub-storage, preserving the other fields
      and elements. The descriptors are storage operands, not computation values or call arguments.
      The following notation is descriptive, not a finalized text grammar:</p>
      <pre><code>root  = LocalReference(containerRegister)
cells = MemberReference(root, "cells", arrayType)
cell  = IndexReference(cells, indexRegister, recordType)
field = MemberReference(cell, "value", int)
store field, replacementValue
result : int = load field</code></pre>

      <h3 id="partial-write-availability">6.2 Local aggregate availability</h3>
      <p>Local availability is tracked for whole registers, not independently for every field or
      element. A local aggregate must be wholly assigned before any derived load or partial store.
      Construct, copy, whole-slot load, or whole-local store can establish that assignment.</p>
      <p>A partial store shall not establish whole-register assignment: writing one field does not
      initialize its siblings, even if the producer later writes more fields. This conservative
      rule avoids implicit partial-initialization tracking. Parameter values are supplied in full
      at invocation; their mutable sub-storage follows the normal parameter permissions. Stage
      output initialization follows the finer-grained rules in section 6.3.</p>

      <h3 id="output-definedness">6.3 Output declarations and definedness</h3>
      <p>A vertex stage shall declare its Position output, and a fragment stage its primary
      FragmentColor output, even if it always discards. Every declared output must be fully assigned
      on each normal entry-function return. No output is initially assigned. A read requires prior
      definite assignment on every incoming path. Termination itself is not proved.</p>
      <p>Output records and fixed arrays are split recursively into observable leaves; scalar,
      vector, matrix, and sampler values are each one leaf. Empty records have no leaves. Whole
      stores establish all selected leaves; member and constant-index stores establish their
      selected subranges without requiring assigned siblings. Dynamic-index reads require every
      potentially selected leaf, and dynamic-index stores establish no new definite leaf, even
      for one-element arrays. These output rules are distinct from the whole-local rule in 6.2.</p>
      <p>Each helper has output read-before-write prerequisites, guaranteed assignments on normal
      returns, and a normal-return possibility. A caller must satisfy read prerequisites and receives
      those guarantees on normal continuation. Discarded paths do not contribute to return guarantees;
      reads before discard remain checked. A helper with no normal return cuts the caller's remaining
      instructions and successors for availability checks. All syntactic instructions still receive
      structural/type validation. Direct and indirect recursion, even in unused functions or
      unreachable blocks, are rejected. Summaries use acyclic call-graph order and loop must fixed
      points. Both conditional successors are considered, even for constant conditions.</p>

      <h2 id="instructions">7. Instructions</h2>
      <p>Each result-producing operation has an explicit destination register. Three-address does
      not mean every operation has exactly three operands: calls and constructors have ordered
      operand lists, while loads, stores, and control transfers have their own shapes. Each operation
      consumes simple operands; nested source-language expression trees are outside canonical VPL.</p>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Instruction</th><th>Fields</th><th>Semantics</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><code>DeclareLocalInstruction</code></td><td>registerValue, type</td><td>Declare a register of that type; no initialization or runtime value computation.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>CopyInstruction</code></td><td>result, source</td><td>Assign a value of exactly the result's type. Copying a reference is not an implicit load.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>LoadInstruction</code></td><td>result, source reference</td><td>Read root or derived storage into a register of the same type.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>StoreInstruction</code></td><td>target reference, value</td><td>Write an exactly matching value to writable root or derived storage. No result register.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>BinaryInstruction</code></td><td>result, operatorKind, lhs, rhs</td><td>Compute typed arithmetic or a scalar comparison.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>CallInstruction</code></td><td>result?, calleeFunction?, builtin, arguments</td><td>Call a same-stage function or a supported built-in with signature-matching values.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>ConstructInstruction</code></td><td>result, type, arguments</td><td>Explicitly construct a value or perform a supported scalar conversion.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>SwizzleInstruction</code></td><td>result, source, pattern</td><td>Extract 1 to 4 xyzw components from a vector, in pattern order.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>MemberInstruction</code></td><td>result, source, fieldName</td><td>Extract an existing record field with its declared type.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>IndexInstruction</code></td><td>result, source, index</td><td>Extract an array element using an integer scalar index. Literal indices must be in bounds.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <h3 id="arithmetic">7.1 Arithmetic and comparisons</h3>
      <p>Add, Subtract, Multiply, and Divide require numeric operands. Matching scalars/vectors
      produce the same type; vector operations are component-wise. Matching matrices support
      addition, subtraction, and component-wise division. Matrix multiplication requires matching
      inner dimensions and produces the outer dimensions; matrix-times-vector produces a vector
      with the matrix's row count.</p>
      <p>A floating vector or matrix may be multiplied or divided by a floating scalar, retaining
      its type. Floating scalar multiplication in the opposite order is also supported.
      Vector-times-matrix and other mixed arithmetic types are currently rejected. Numeric-to-Boolean
      conversion is not implicit. Integer-to-float conversion shall be an explicit construction
      before arithmetic, assignment, calls, or returns requiring float.</p>
      <p>Equal and NotEqual accept matching Boolean, integer, or floating scalars and produce bool.
      Less, LessEqual, Greater, and GreaterEqual require matching numeric scalars and produce bool.
      Composite comparisons are not defined. Floating special-value comparisons follow section 4.1.</p>
      <h3 id="construction">7.2 Construction and conversion</h3>
      <p>A scalar construction takes one operand of the same scalar type, or an integer operand
      for a floating result. This explicitly represents integer widening. Floating-to-integer and
      numeric-to-Boolean conversions are not currently supported.</p>
      <p>A vector construction supplies matching scalar/vector components totaling its dimension,
      or one matching scalar to fill every component. Floating vector/matrix construction also
      accepts integer scalar components as an explicit part of that construction. Matrix components
      are supplied in column-major order, totaling rows times columns; one scalar instead forms a
      diagonal matrix with zero off-diagonal entries.</p>
      <p>An array construction supplies exactly its element count, with each operand matching the
      element type. Record construction supplies one exactly matching operand per field, in field
      order. Empty record construction is permitted. Samplers cannot be constructed.</p>
      <h3 id="calls">7.3 Calls</h3>
      <p>A function call shall select a non-NULL <code>calleeFunction</code> and
      <code>BuiltinFunction_None</code>. Its operand count and types shall match the ordered
      parameters. A non-void callee requires a matching destination register; a void callee requires
      NULL result. For a built-in call, the function reference shall be NULL and the selector shall
      be supported. The current built-in is <code>Texture(sampler2D, vec2) -&gt; vec4</code>, with
      floating vectors.</p>
      <h3 id="extraction">7.4 Extraction</h3>
      <p>A swizzle's pattern shall contain only xyzw selectors valid for the source dimension.
      One selector produces a scalar of the source element kind; multiple selectors produce a
      vector of that kind and pattern length. Repeated components are allowed for extraction.</p>
      <p>A member operation requires a record value and a declared field name. An index operation
      requires an array value and integer index. Both produce values of the selected element type,
      not storage addresses; use MemberReferenceValue/IndexReferenceValue for storage access.
      Dynamic out-of-bounds access has undefined execution behavior as in section 6.1.</p>

      <h2 id="control-flow">8. Control flow</h2>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Terminator</th><th>Fields</th><th>Effect</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><code>BranchTerminator</code></td><td>target</td><td>Transfer to a block in the same function.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>ConditionalBranchTerminator</code></td><td>condition, trueTarget, falseTarget</td><td>Test a bool value and transfer to one of the two same-function blocks.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>ReturnTerminator</code></td><td>value?</td><td>Finish the function. Void returns require NULL value; non-void returns require an exactly matching value.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>DiscardTerminator</code></td><td>None</td><td>Abandon the fragment invocation, with no successor and no returned value, regardless of function return type.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>Discard is fragment-only and terminates a block. The older <code>DiscardInstruction</code>
      scaffold is rejected by canonical verification; producers shall use the terminator form.</p>
      <p>Loops are explicit back-edges. Higher-level if/while/for/break/continue statements are
      expressed by graph construction in the producing tool or future external language frontend.
      The following version-1 function fragment shows mutable registers and a loop:</p>
      <pre><code>(registers (register r0 IntType) (register r1 BoolType))
(block entry
  (copy r0 (int 0))
  (branch test))
(block test
  (less r1 r0 (int 10))
  (branch_if r1 body exit))
(block body
  (add r0 r0 (int 1))
  (branch test))
(block exit (return r0))</code></pre>
      <p>The enclosing function in this sketch returns int. Removing the initial copy makes its
      first comparison invalid: assignment on the loop back-edge does not define r0 on first entry.</p>

      <h2 id="verification">9. Verification</h2>
      <p><code>Arcadia_VPL_IR_Verifier_run(thread, verifier, module)</code> checks a current module
      graph without the legacy AST or symbol phases. It reports the first invalid object through a
      <code>VerificationDiagnostic</code> containing a message and an optional subject object, and
      raises <code>Arcadia_Status_SemanticalError</code>. It releases scratch memory and restores
      the value stack on success or failure. The same verifier can be used for subsequent runs.</p>
      <p>Object constructors do not establish all canonical invariants. Direct builders and
      parsed text shall use the same verifier. A successful check applies to that graph at that
      moment; mutations require re-verification. Immutable publication through VerifiedModule
      separates saved compiler input from mutable builders and fresh materializations (section 11).</p>
      <p><code>Arcadia.VPL.Tests.Verifier</code> covers branch joins, repeated writes, first-iteration
      loop availability, register/type consistency, storage permissions, calls including void calls,
      cyclic types, fragment discard, arrays of records, constructors, swizzles, texture sampling,
      nested storage paths, inherited permissions, cyclic references, partial-write initialization,
      selector availability, constructor rejection, reruns, and failure cleanup. Construction tests
      remain in <code>Arcadia.VPL.Tests.IR</code>.
      Legacy GLSL golden tests check the four existing engine variants during migration.</p>

      @{include("./text-format.i")}
      @{include("./glsl-backend.i")}

      <h2 id="pending">13. Remaining specification work</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Additional targets/profiles, hardware-limit validation, legacy frontend retirement,
        and externalizing the built-in canonical template data.</li>
        <li>Future language extensions and format compatibility beyond version 1.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Target-neutral validity alone does not imply support by every backend. The migration
      checklist and register-based control-flow rationale are recorded in
      <code>VPL/Library/Sources/Arcadia/VPL/DESIGN.md</code>.</p>
    </div>
